# Bulletproof React conventions

Frontend apps follow [Bulletproof React](https://github.com/alan2207/bulletproof-react). Place new files by these rules.

## Layout

```text
src/
├── app/          Routes and layouts — thin
├── components/   Shared UI used across features
├── config/       Env and constants (non-JSX)
├── features/     Feature modules — most code lives here
│   └── <feature>/
│       ├── api/          Data access for this feature
│       ├── components/
│       ├── hooks/
│       ├── stores/
│       ├── types/
│       ├── utils/        Pure functions — unit-test these
│       └── index.ts      Public API
├── hooks/        Shared hooks only
├── lib/          Preconfigured clients (HTTP, query, web3)
├── types/
└── utils/
```

## Rules

- **Thin routes** — route files compose features; no data fetching or business logic inline.
- **Feature public API** — import across features only through `features/<x>/index.ts`, never reach into internals.
- **Logic out of components** — domain and math logic in `features/*/utils` or `lib/` so it tests without rendering; hooks wrap IO.
- **Shared means shared** — code used by one feature belongs to that feature, not `components/`.
- **No parallel layer schemes** — don't add `screens/`, `widgets/`, `entities/`, or a Pages-Router `pages/`.

Check placement before logic. Features that import each other's internals get hard to move or delete later.
